Microsoft Learn: Manage and Control Traffic Flow in Your Azure Deployment with Routes

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Manage and Control Traffic Flow in Your Azure Deployment with Routes
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how Azure routing works, how to implement custom routes, and how to control network traffic flow using route tables and Network Virtual Appliances (NVAs).

This module focused on Azure routing capabilities, User-Defined Routes (UDRs), Network Virtual Appliances, and directing traffic through custom network paths to improve security and network control.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Routing

The module introduced routing as the process Azure uses to determine how network traffic travels between resources and networks.

Every packet sent within Azure follows a route.

Azure automatically creates system routes to allow communication between:

Virtual Machines
Virtual Networks
Azure Services
Internet destinations
On-premises networks
Benefits of Azure Routing:
Automatic connectivity
Simplified networking
Dynamic route management
Hybrid connectivity support

The module emphasized that Azure automatically manages routing unless administrators choose to customize it.

Step 2: Understanding Azure System Routes

The training covered Azure's built-in system routes.

System routes are automatically created and maintained by Azure.

Examples of system routes:
Destination	Next Hop
Virtual Network	Local VNet
Internet	Internet
Virtual Appliance	Custom appliance
VPN Gateway	Virtual Network Gateway
Key concepts learned:
System routes require no configuration
Azure manages route updates automatically
System routes provide default connectivity

The module highlighted that system routes form the foundation of Azure networking.

Step 3: Understanding User-Defined Routes (UDRs)

A major focus of the module was User-Defined Routes.

User-Defined Routes allow administrators to override Azure's default routing behavior.

Benefits:
Traffic inspection
Network segmentation
Security enforcement
Centralized traffic control
Example:

Default route:

VM → Internet

Custom route:

VM → Firewall → Internet
Key lessons learned:
UDRs override system routes
Route tables store custom routes
UDRs provide greater control over traffic flow

The module demonstrated how custom routes support advanced networking architectures.

Step 4: Understanding Route Tables

The training introduced Azure Route Tables.

Route tables contain one or more custom routes that define traffic paths.

Route Table Components:
Component	Purpose
Address Prefix	Destination network
Next Hop Type	Routing destination
Next Hop Address	Target IP address
Example Route:
Address Prefix	Next Hop
0.0.0.0/0	Firewall Appliance

This route sends all traffic to a firewall before leaving the network.

Benefits:
Centralized route management
Traffic inspection
Custom network design

The module emphasized that route tables are associated with subnets.

